        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/d/3e96dc/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2014-11-01T23:51:34.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I was playing a game with Parse and Nightmist, and it's raised some questions about exactly how flexible Syntacic Analysis is. &nbsp;The card text:</p><p>\"Select a player. &nbsp;That player reveals the top 2 cards of their deck. &nbsp;They may play, draw, or discard each of those cards in any order.\"</p><p>Now, I've always played this card with the assumption that the word \"each\" means the selected player can choose a different option for each card. &nbsp;But, now it occurs to me that the word \"may\" implies that the player is not required to choose any of those 3 options, in which case the card would return to the top of the deck, since it has only been revealed. &nbsp;This is particularly relevant to Nightmist because of cards like Enlightenment which check the spell icon value of the top card of her deck.</p><p>So, for example, let's say Nightmist drew an Enlightenment (1) and a Tome of Elder Magic (4). &nbsp;I think she chould choose to process the Tome first, pass on all 3 options to return it to the top of the deck, then play Enlightenment to give someone a guaranteed 4 card draw; or play the Enlightenment first to gamble on the draw (since the Tome is still being revealed, it's not technically on top of the deck), then return the Tome to the top of the deck to power one the spells in her hand on her next turn; or choose to pass on both cards to guarantee an optimized Oblivion on her next turn.</p><p>But, I feel like I'm making some assumptions with this interpretation, so does anyone see a problem with it? &nbsp;Thanks.</p>",
        "post_number": 1,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T01:42:47.250Z",
