        "raw": "<p>I was playing a game with Parse and Nightmist, and it&#39;s raised some questions about exactly how flexible Syntacic Analysis is. &nbsp;The card text:</p><p>&quot;Select a player. &nbsp;That player reveals the top 2 cards of their deck. &nbsp;They may play, draw, or discard each of those cards in any order.&quot;</p><p>Now, I&#39;ve always played this card with the assumption that the word &quot;each&quot; means the selected player can choose a different option for each card. &nbsp;But, now it occurs to me that the word &quot;may&quot; implies that the player is not required to choose any of those 3 options, in which case the card would return to the top of the deck, since it has only been revealed. &nbsp;This is particularly relevant to Nightmist because of cards like Enlightenment which check the spell icon value of the top card of her deck.</p><p>So, for example, let&#39;s say Nightmist drew an Enlightenment (1) and a Tome of Elder Magic (4). &nbsp;I think she chould choose to process the Tome first, pass on all 3 options to return it to the top of the deck, then play Enlightenment to give someone a guaranteed 4 card draw; or play the Enlightenment first to gamble on the draw (since the Tome is still being revealed, it&#39;s not technically on top of the deck), then return the Tome to the top of the deck to power one the spells in her hand on her next turn; or choose to pass on both cards to guarantee an optimized Oblivion on her next turn.</p><p>But, I feel like I&#39;m making some assumptions with this interpretation, so does anyone see a problem with it? &nbsp;Thanks.</p>",
