      {
        "id": 119934,
        "name": "Sefirit",
        "username": "Sefirit",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/s/e68b1a/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2014-11-03T16:39:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Honestly I think he did read it literally.&nbsp; Don't we have cards that say something like \"You may draw a card\" which is ready as \"you can draw a card or not as you choose.\"&nbsp; I'm sure matchstick is correct on his ruling, but as far as reading it literally I could see an argument being made for</p><p>1. card is revealed</p><p>2. I may draw it, but choose not to</p><p>3. I may play it, but choose not to</p><p>4. I may discard it, but choose not to</p><p>5. nothing happens to card i.e. usually when you only reveal a card and don't do anything else with it, it is returned to the top of the deck.</p><p>I suppose you could say that it never tells you to return it to the top so it would sit in some ambiguous revealed state forever.&nbsp; I suppose you might also need a clause in there to say that if any option is chosen no other option can be chosen for the same card.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>I'm not a fan of rules lawyering in most cases but this seemed clever.&nbsp; I like it better than some things I've seen in other posts such as Freedom Six Tempest being able to destroy his cards under La Capitan.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
